---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Verdict from '../components/Verdict.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
import { REPO_URL, REPO_BENCH_PATH } from '../../site.config.mjs';
import { ARMS, ARMSETS, FAMILY_LABEL, HYPOTHESES, TIERS } from '../lib/prereg';
import {
  ADMISSION_CRITERIA,
  ANALYSIS_PLAN,
  BASELINE_RULES,
  BLINDING_RULES,
  COST_IS_A_RESULT,
  DISCOVERY_CAVEAT,
  EXCLUSION_RULE,
  GATES_NOT_IN_BUNDLE,
  HEADROOM_BANDS,
  INTEGRITY_GATES,
  JUDGE_POLICY,
  MDE_TABLE,
  MECHANISMS,
  MECHANISM_TEST,
  MOCK_RULE,
  NARRATION_RULE,
  NOT_USEFUL_COMMITMENTS,
  PINNING,
  PRIOR_WORK,
  RECORD_FIELDS,
  SECONDARY_ENDPOINTS,
  THREATS,
  VERDICT_RULES,
  VIDEO_TIERS,
  WHAT_IS_NEW,
} from '../lib/methods';
import type { Verdict as VerdictKind } from '../lib/types';

const prereg = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/docs/preregistration.md`;
const armsPy = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${REPO_BENCH_PATH}/harness/v2sbench/arms.py`;

const toc = [
  ['design', 'The design'],
  ['prior-work', 'What is already known'],
  ['arms', 'The arms'],
  ['suite', 'The task suite'],
  ['headroom', 'Headroom'],
  ['gates', 'Integrity gates'],
  ['materials', 'Materials'],
  ['analysis', 'Statistical analysis plan'],
  ['verdicts', 'How a verdict is decided'],
  ['records', 'Records and pinning'],
  ['mock', 'Mock data'],
  ['threats', 'Threats to validity'],
  ['not-useful', 'What would make us say v2s is not useful'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="The frozen design behind v2s-bench: the arms and what each isolates, the task tiers, the headroom calibration, the statistical analysis plan, and the threats to validity."
>
  <div class="stack">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Pre-registered design</p>
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Written and committed before any trial was run, and not edited after the freeze
        commit except by appended, dated amendments. Its purpose is to make the result
        falsifiable: the hypotheses, the task set, the exclusion rule, the primary
        endpoint and the statistical test are all fixed while the outcome is still
        unknown. The authority is the
        <a href={prereg}>pre-registration</a> itself; this page is its reading copy.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        If you are reading a results page that does not link to a <em>frozen</em> copy of
        that file, treat the numbers as exploratory.
      </p>
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <ul>
          {toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>

    <section aria-labelledby="design">
      <h2 id="design">The design</h2>
      <p>
        The repository already measures whether the <em>extraction</em> is faithful:
        <code>vts-extract eval</code> scores boundary and step F1 and command recall
        against a synthetic gold set. That is an intrinsic measure &mdash; it asks whether
        the pipeline read the video correctly. v2s-bench asks the extrinsic question,
        which nothing else in the project answers.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        Does an agent holding a v2s-generated skill complete real tasks more often than
        the same agent with a good prompt and no skill &mdash; and which component of v2s
        is responsible?
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Being faithful is not the same as being useful. A pipeline can score well on
        extraction and change no outcome, because the model already knew the procedure,
        or because the environment corrects the agent anyway, or because the skill is
        written in a way an agent does not act on.
      </p>

      <h3>The hypotheses</h3>
      <p class="note">
        The pre-registration does not carry a standalone hypothesis table. H1 and the
        ablations are defined by the <code>COMPARISONS</code> tuple in
        <a href={armsPy}>arms.py</a>, which is what the analysis code tests; H2 and H7
        are defined by the sections that test them, because neither is a paired two-arm
        difference. Which is which is stated below rather than presenting all eight as
        the same kind of claim.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">The pre-registered hypotheses.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">#</th>
            <th scope="col">Claim</th>
            <th scope="col">Evidence</th>
            <th scope="col">Fails if</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            HYPOTHESES.map((h) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {h.id}
                  <span class="sub">{FAMILY_LABEL[h.family]}</span>
                </th>
                <td data-label="Claim">
                  {h.statement}
                  {h.note && <span class="sub">{h.note}</span>}
                </td>
                <td data-label="Evidence">
                  {h.evidence}
                  <span class="sub">{h.source}</span>
                </td>
                <td data-label="Fails if">{h.failsIf}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="note">
        H1 alone is a weak claim and is not reported alone. &ldquo;Give the model the
        answer, it does better&rdquo; is nearly tautological; H3 to H6 are what separate
        v2s from a transcript summariser, and H2 is what tells a reader when the tool is
        worth running at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="prior-work">
      <h2 id="prior-work">What is already known, and what is actually new here</h2>
      <p>
        This is not an unexplored question, and the benchmark is designed around what the
        adjacent literature already settles. Each row below changed a design decision.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Prior findings, their sources, and the consequence for this design.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Finding</th>
            <th scope="col">Source</th>
            <th scope="col">Consequence for this design</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            PRIOR_WORK.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.finding}</th>
                <td data-label="Source">{row.source}</td>
                <td data-label="Consequence">{row.consequence}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3>What is new</h3>
      <ol class="spaced">
        {WHAT_IS_NEW.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="arms">
      <h2 id="arms">The arms, and what each isolates</h2>
      <p>
        All arms share the same model, tool access, sandbox, turn and time budget, retry
        policy and task prompt. <strong>The only difference is the context material
        supplied.</strong> Arms run paired: every (task, seed) is attempted by every arm
        in the run&rsquo;s arm set, and arm order is interleaved within a seed.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          The experimental arms, the context each supplies, and the confound or component
          each isolates.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Arm</th>
            <th scope="col">Context supplied</th>
            <th scope="col">Isolates</th>
            <th scope="col">Arm sets</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            ARMS.map((arm) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {arm.id}
                  <span class="sub">{arm.name}</span>
                </th>
                <td data-label="Context supplied">
                  {arm.supplies}
                  <span class="sub">{arm.description}</span>
                </td>
                <td data-label="Isolates">{arm.isolates}</td>
                <td data-label="Arm sets">
                  <span class="chip chip--square">{arm.armsets.join(' ')}</span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="note">{DISCOVERY_CAVEAT}</p>

      <h3>Arm sets</h3>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          ARMSETS.map((set) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                <code>{set.id}</code> &middot; {set.arms.join(', ')}
              </dt>
              <dd>{set.purpose}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h3>The baseline must be strong</h3>
      <p>
        A weak baseline is the standard way to manufacture a benchmark win. Three rules,
        enforced in the harness rather than by good intentions.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          BASELINE_RULES.map((rule) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{rule.title}</dt>
              <dd>{rule.body}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h3>Arm-material authoring is grader-blind</h3>
      <p>
        The most likely way to accidentally fake a result is to let the procedure text be
        shaped by knowledge of how success is scored. So that is prevented structurally.
      </p>
      <ul class="spaced">
        {BLINDING_RULES.map((rule) => <li>{rule}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="suite">
      <h2 id="suite">The task suite</h2>
      <p>
        Four tiers, easiest to hardest. Each task is a directory with a
        <code>task.yaml</code>, a <code>check/</code> directory of execution-based
        assertions, an <code>oracle/</code> solution, and a declared knowledge gap: the
        specific fact or ordering required for success that the video teaches.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">The four task tiers.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Tier</th>
            <th scope="col">Environment</th>
            <th scope="col">Why it is here</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Target n</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            TIERS.map((tier) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {tier.label}
                  <span class="sub">{tier.name}</span>
                </th>
                <td data-label="Environment">{tier.environment}</td>
                <td data-label="Why it is here">{tier.why}</td>
                <td class="num" data-label="Target n">{tier.targetN}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="note">
        Counts are targets, frozen at the freeze commit. The sample size the primary
        endpoint actually requires is larger; v0.1 publishes a pilot and states its power
        honestly rather than pretending a 42-task suite settles a 5 pp question.
      </p>

      <h3>Tier 1 is the internal-validity anchor</h3>
      <p>
        Benchmarking v2s on <code>git init</code> or <code>:q!</code> measures nothing:
        every current model knows those cold, the baseline scores about 100%, and no
        skill can move it. So T1 invents its own tools &mdash; small, real, working CLIs
        installed in the task container with deliberately non-guessable interfaces: a
        token read from a file, an argument order that matters, a two-phase commit, a
        subcommand whose obvious name does not exist. The agent drives the real tool;
        only the tool&rsquo;s provenance is synthetic.
      </p>
      <p>
        Half the T1 tools ship good <code>--help</code> and half ship poor or terse
        documentation, declared per task as <code>discoverability</code>. We expect v2s to
        matter little in the first group and more in the second, and that split is more
        useful than any aggregate.
      </p>

      <h3>Environments are Docker-only, by necessity</h3>
      <p>
        The two best-known desktop suites boot a QEMU VM inside the container and
        silently fall back to software emulation when <code>/dev/kvm</code> is absent:
        they &ldquo;work&rdquo; and are uselessly slow. Standard CI runners offer no
        reliable nested virtualisation and about 14 GB of disk. So T3 is container-native:
        real applications under Xvfb, plus browser tasks driven through a browser
        automation library. A hypervisor-backed desktop tier is explicitly out of scope
        for v0.1 rather than attempted badly.
      </p>

      <h3>Admission criteria</h3>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          ADMISSION_CRITERIA.map((c) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{c.title}</dt>
              <dd>{c.body}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h3>A calibration rung</h3>
      <p>
        Published work fed a model the paper describing an environment and beat every
        reinforcement-learning baseline with no interaction at all &mdash; a large,
        independently reported effect of documentation on agent behaviour. One T4 task
        reproduces that setup as a harness calibration check: if the harness cannot
        recover a known-large effect, its null results are not evidence of anything. It is
        reported as a harness diagnostic, never as a v2s result.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="headroom">
      <h2 id="headroom">Headroom, and the knowing&ndash;doing gap</h2>
      <p>
        Each task&rsquo;s knowledge gap is probed directly before the main run: the key
        fact is asked of the model as a plain question, ten times, with no tools and no
        skill. The pass rate is the task&rsquo;s <code>probe_known</code> &mdash; its
        measured headroom, published per task.
      </p>
      <p>
        Publishing the probes is also the honest disclosure that <em>any</em> skill
        system&rsquo;s effect size depends on what the model already knew, and that a
        benchmark can be made to show almost any effect by choosing tasks on that axis.
        The probe is also a direct measurement of the knowing&ndash;doing gap: a task with
        high <code>probe_known</code> and low baseline success is one the model can state
        and cannot do.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">Headroom bands and their thresholds.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Band</th>
            <th scope="col">Rule</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            HEADROOM_BANDS.map((band) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>{band.band}</code></th>
                <td data-label="Rule">{band.rule}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Feedback bandwidth</h3>
      <p>
        The published null attributes its result to the environment already correcting the
        agent: when a tool emits a clear error, trial-and-error substitutes for
        instruction. Each task therefore declares <code>feedback_bandwidth</code> &mdash;
        whether a wrong attempt produces a diagnostic error (high) or silently produces a
        plausible-looking wrong result (low). We expect the effect to concentrate in
        <code>low</code>, and we test it.
      </p>

      <h3>Two mechanisms, opposite predictions</h3>
      <p>
        The interesting disagreement in the literature is <em>why</em> skills help, and
        the strata are what separates the two accounts.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          MECHANISMS.map((m) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                {m.id} &middot; {m.name}
              </dt>
              <dd>{m.claim}</dd>
              <dd class="note">{m.predicts}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      {MECHANISM_TEST.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}

      <h3>Exclusion is declared on the probe, never on the outcome</h3>
      {EXCLUSION_RULE.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="gates">
      <h2 id="gates">Integrity gates</h2>
      <p>Run by CI before any trial, and published with the results.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          INTEGRITY_GATES.map((gate) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{gate.title}</dt>
              <dd>{gate.body}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="note">{GATES_NOT_IN_BUNDLE}</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="materials">
      <h2 id="materials">Materials</h2>
      <p>
        Tutorial video is the input to the system under test, which creates a tension
        between reproducibility and realism. Both horns are taken, and which is which is
        labelled rather than picking one and overclaiming.
        <strong>Numbers from the two tiers are never averaged.</strong>
      </p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        {
          VIDEO_TIERS.map((tier) => (
            <div class="panel">
              <h3>
                {tier.name} <span class="chip chip--square">{tier.id}</span>
              </h3>
              <p>{tier.what}</p>
              <p class="note"><strong>Strength.</strong> {tier.strength}</p>
              <p class="note"><strong>Weakness.</strong> {tier.weakness}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <h3>Narration must be vague where the pixels are exact</h3>
      <p>{NARRATION_RULE}</p>
      <h3>Licensing</h3>
      <p>
        No video bytes, no game ROMs, no copyrighted assets in the repository. Real videos
        are CC-BY, CC0 or public-domain only, with the licence recorded per entry and the
        attribution rendered on the <a href={href('/tasks')}>task pages</a>. Games are
        free and open-source only, with assets fetched upstream at build time; commercial
        ROMs are categorically excluded. Adapted third-party task definitions carry their
        upstream licence.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="analysis">
      <h2 id="analysis">Statistical analysis plan</h2>
      <p>
        Fixed in advance. <strong>Primary endpoint:</strong> task success, binary, decided
        by execution-based assertions. A trial succeeds only if every required assertion
        passes.
      </p>

      <h3>Sample size</h3>
      <p>
        Variance decomposes so that only the number of <em>tasks</em> reduces the
        between-task term. At a fixed budget of runs per arm, splitting it into more tasks
        and fewer trials each buys power; we nonetheless run three trials per cell,
        because one cannot detect a flaky task, cannot estimate intra-task correlation,
        and cannot report pass<sup>k</sup>.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--rows mde">
        <caption>
          Minimum detectable effect in percentage points &mdash; <strong>assumed, not
          measured</strong> ({MDE_TABLE.assumptions}).
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="num-left">N tasks</th>
            {MDE_TABLE.columns.map((c) => <th scope="col" class="num">{c}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            MDE_TABLE.rows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" class="num-left">
                  {row.n}
                  {row.target && <span class="sub">target</span>}
                </th>
                {row.values.map((v) => <td class="num">{v.toFixed(1)}</td>)}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p><strong>{MDE_TABLE.target}</strong></p>
      <p>{MDE_TABLE.pilot}</p>
      <p>{MDE_TABLE.difficulty}</p>

      <h3>Tests and intervals</h3>
      <dl class="defs">
        {
          ANALYSIS_PLAN.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{item.title}</dt>
              <dd>{item.body}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h3>Secondary endpoints</h3>
      <p class="note">Reported for every trial, never substituted for the primary.</p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">Secondary endpoints recorded per trial.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Endpoint</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            SECONDARY_ENDPOINTS.map((e) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>{e.name}</code></th>
                <td data-label="What it is">{e.what}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>{COST_IS_A_RESULT}</p>

      <h3>On LLM judges</h3>
      <ul class="spaced">
        {JUDGE_POLICY.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="verdicts">
      <h2 id="verdicts">How a verdict is decided</h2>
      <p>
        Verdicts are computed from the interval and the pre-registered failure condition,
        never written by hand. All four render at the same visual weight. A null result is
        a result: <strong>not supported</strong> is not styled as a warning, and the
        benchmark commits to publishing it.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs verdict-rules">
        {
          VERDICT_RULES.map((rule) => (
            <div>
              <dt><Verdict verdict={rule.verdict as VerdictKind} /></dt>
              <dd>{rule.rule}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="records">
      <h2 id="records">Records and pinning</h2>
      <p>{RECORD_FIELDS}</p>
      <p>{PINNING}</p>
      <p>
        The harness&rsquo;s <code>verify-results</code> command recomputes every published
        figure from the raw records, so a reader can audit the arithmetic rather than
        trusting it. How to run it is on <a href={href('/reproduce')}>reproduce</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="mock">
      <h2 id="mock">Mock data can never become a published figure</h2>
      {MOCK_RULE.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="threats">
      <h2 id="threats">Threats to validity</h2>
      <p>
        Stated here because a benchmark whose authors also build the tool under test has
        to be read with suspicion, and the only answer to that is to name the ways it
        could be wrong.
      </p>
      <table class="dt dt--stack dt--rows">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Threats to validity, their mitigations, and the residual risk that remains.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Threat</th>
            <th scope="col">Mitigation</th>
            <th scope="col">Residual risk</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            THREATS.map((t) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{t.threat}</th>
                <td data-label="Mitigation">{t.mitigation}</td>
                <td data-label="Residual risk">{t.residual}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="not-useful">
      <h2 id="not-useful">What would make us say v2s is not useful</h2>
      <p>Committed in advance, before any data existed.</p>
      <ul class="spaced">
        {NOT_USEFUL_COMMITMENTS.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="note">
        Amendments to any of the above are dated, appended and never rewritten, and each
        records what changed, why, and whether any data had been seen at the time.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .toc {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--grid);
    padding-top: 0.8rem;
  }

  .toc ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.3rem 1rem;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .toc li {
    max-width: none;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.95rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--series-1);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .spaced li + li {
    margin-top: 0.45rem;
  }

  .mde caption {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

@media (min-width: 761px) {
    .mde th[scope='row'] {
      width: 12ch;
    }
}

  .verdict-rules dt {
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  section h3 {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
